<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=codex">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Codex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A codex is a book of pages stacked and bound at one edge, the ancestor of the modern book, first described in the 1st century CE, when the Roman poet Martial praised its convenience. The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> developed it from wax tablets, and early codices were mostly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a>, later of parchment. Early Christians favoured it from the 2nd century; the codex equalled the scroll in number around 300 CE and had replaced it across the Christianised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-roman-world">Greco-Roman world</a> by the 6th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codex.jpg" alt="Codex" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Palauenc05 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egbert_codex.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1st century CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Rome</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>A papyrus codex from Ptolemaic Egypt may be older.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus<span>c. 3000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=codex">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
